---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import FamilyLine from '../components/FamilyLine.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import FeatureList from '../components/FeatureList.astro';
import { CURRENT, IOT_FEATURES, STATUS, product } from '../lib/products';

const p = product('iot');
---

<Base
  title="HotLoop IoT: the automation base, built in Go for OT"
  description="HotLoop IoT is the automation base for the plant floor, written in Go, with MQTT discovery. 4.18.10 is out. Free for home and lab."
  ogAlt="HotLoop IoT, the automation base, built in Go for OT."
>
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={{
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: p.name,
      url: 'https://hotloop.io/iot/',
      description:
        'An automation base for operational technology, written natively in Go: entities, automations, helpers, scripts, the logbook, dashboards, alarms, notifications, MCP, and MQTT with discovery, plus industrial protocol drivers.',
      applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Linux',
      license: 'https://hotloop.io/license/',
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      softwareVersion: CURRENT,
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://hotloop.io/#organization' },
    }}
  />
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="cluster"><span class="label">HotLoop IoT</span><span class="chip ok">{CURRENT} is out</span></div>
      <h1>The automation base for the plant floor.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Entities, automations, helpers, scripts, the logbook, dashboards, notifications and MCP, on MQTT with
        discovery, plus every industrial driver HotLoop has. Written from scratch in Go, one static binary. It's the
        box for your garage, your greenhouse, your small shop, and the used PLC you bought off eBay because you
        couldn't help yourself.
      </p>
      <div class="notice info">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" /><path d="M12 8h.01M11 12h1v5h1" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>HotLoop IoT {CURRENT} is out. It's the first release of IoT, and it ships beside the Gateway, Edge and the Edge Relay from the same tag.</strong>
          <p>
            The image is <code>{p.image}:{CURRENT}</code> for amd64, arm64 and arm/v7, about 29 MB on arm, and it pulls
            with no login. The chart is <code>{p.chart}</code> from <code>https://hotloop.io/hotloop</code>. MQTT
            discovery was the condition for shipping it, and it's in: IoT finds your Shelly, ESPHome, Tasmota and
            Zigbee2MQTT gear by itself instead of making you type topics.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cluster">
        <a class="btn primary" href="#install">Install it</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/products/">Compare the lineup</a>
        <a class="btn" href="https://docs.hotloop.io/iot/overview/">Docs</a>
      </div>
      <FamilyLine id="iot" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap grid grid-2 split">
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">Why it exists</span>
        <h2>Everything is an entity, and everything else is built on that.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          A tag is what a driver talks about: a register, a NodeId, a DB offset. An entity is what people talk
          about: the zone 1 temperature, the conveyor run switch. IoT puts every signal behind an entity whose id
          never changes. Somebody deletes the tag and re-imports it during a PLC change? The entity comes back with
          its id, so the rule that says <code>sensor.oven_temperature</code> keeps working instead of quietly dying.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          Every line is ours, written in Go, for OT. Nothing embedded, bridged or forked, so when something breaks at
          3am there's one codebase to read and nobody else's bug to wait on. Every command goes through the one write
          gate, and a failed read is bad quality, never a zero.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">The base of the lineup</span>
        <h2>Everything above IoT is IoT plus more.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          <a href="/edge/">Edge</a> is IoT plus the machine layer. The <a href="/gateway/">Gateway</a> is everything
          plus fleet, multi-site and reports. The <a href="/edge-relay/">Edge Relay</a> is the headless one. One
          codebase, one tag, four images, released together, so a fix in IoT is a fix in all of them the same day.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          Its database is <strong>{p.databases}</strong>, picked by a setting, in the same image. SQLite for the Pi
          on the shelf with no second container, PostgreSQL when it grows up. IoT keeps 30 days of history by
          default, because Pi disks are small. Moving up to Edge or the Gateway keeps your data and your config, and
          it has been run on the Quadlet units, so starting small doesn't paint you into a corner.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">What is in it</span>
        <h2>Each piece says where it actually stands.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          "{STATUS.released.label}" means it ships in the published release, and you can run it tonight.
          "{STATUS.unverified.label}" means the path exists and nobody has pointed it at the real thing yet.
          "{STATUS.building.label}" means somebody is writing it right now. "{STATUS.planned.label}" means exactly
          that, and no amount of squinting turns it into a feature.
        </p>
      </div>
      <FeatureList features={IOT_FEATURES} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="install">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Install</span>
        <h2>Helm on a cluster, Quadlet on a box.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          One chart repository holds all four charts, so adding it once is enough. Call the release
          {' '}<code>hotloop</code> whichever product it is: the release name is what the database volume, the Secrets
          and the Service are named after, and keeping it is what lets a site move up a product later.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2 split">
        <div class="stack">
          <h3>Helm</h3>
<pre><code>helm repo add hotloop https://hotloop.io/hotloop
helm repo update
helm install hotloop hotloop/hotloop-iot --version {CURRENT} \
  --namespace hotloop --create-namespace</code></pre>
          <p class="muted">
            The chart runs PostgreSQL 16 by default. <code>database.type</code> takes <code>postgresql</code> or
            {' '}<code>sqlite</code>, and asking the IoT chart for <code>timescaledb</code> fails the install, because
            IoT doesn't offer it. SQLite means no database container at all, which is what a Pi wants.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <h3>Quadlet</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            For a Pi or a small PC outside any cluster. The IoT units are under <code>deploy/quadlet/iot/</code> in
            the <code>hotloop</code> repository, and <code>deploy/quadlet/iot/sqlite/</code> is the same thing with the
            database as a file. That repository is private for now, so the
            {' '}<a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/install/quadlet/">Quadlet install guide</a> is where to read how they
            work. They need Podman 5.0 or newer, so a Pi wants Raspberry Pi OS Trixie or newer, because Bookworm ships 4.3, which has no Quadlet at all.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            The app follows <code>:stable</code>, and a release that can't start is rolled back on its own. Put the
            database on a USB SSD and not the SD card, because history writes all day and SD cards die of exactly that.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="prose muted after">
        Open <code>http://localhost:8080</code> once it's up, sign in, add a device. Then notice you can't write to
        anything. That's on purpose: every tag starts read-only and gets armed one at a time.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Not proven yet</span>
        <h2>What we haven't run, so you don't find out at 2am.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>The Quadlet units under systemd</h3><p>IoT's SQLite unit was run. The PostgreSQL one was generated from the Gateway's, whose units were run rootless under systemd, and checked against them, but it was not started. A real Pi under systemd as root: not yet.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Real devices on a bench</h3><p>MQTT discovery is proven against recordings of real installs, not against devices on a bench. ESPHome's <code>mqtt:</code> component doesn't build for a host, so there is no live ESPHome in the tests.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>The 32-bit arm image</h3><p>The arm/v7 image is built and published. It hasn't met a Pi 3 under load yet.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="card cta reveal">
        <div class="stack">
          <span class="label">Licensing</span>
          <h2>Free, with no license key.</h2>
          <p class="muted">
            IoT is under the HotLoop Business Source License 1.1, like everything else HotLoop makes. Home and lab
            use is free. Business use is free through <a href="https://embernet.ai">EmberNET</a>. No tiers, no
            key, and no email six months in asking you to upgrade.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn primary" href="/license/">Read the license</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--hl-s7), 7vw, var(--hl-s9)) var(--hl-s7); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 20ch; }
  .page-hero .notice { max-width: 68ch; }
  .split { align-items: start; gap: var(--hl-s8); }
  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--hl-s6); justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: clamp(var(--hl-s6), 5vw, var(--hl-s8)); }
  .cta .stack { max-width: 62ch; }
</style>
